You received a rapid response grant from the National Science Foundation to look at how workers are coping with the abrupt transition to telework during the COVID-19 shutdowns. Can you talk about why you thought it was important to do this research now, how you're doing it, and what questions are you asking? Yeah, so when, I think it was March 13th, when we started realizing that a lot of people were going to be transitioning to remote work, I contacted one of my longtime collaborators at the University of South Florida, Tammy Allen, and said, should we try to get a rapid grant to study this, just because this is sort of a natural experiment like we've never seen before with so many people moving into remote work. So we proposed a study to the National Science Foundation
你获得了美国国家科学基金会的快速响应资助，以研究员工如何应对新冠疫情期间向远程办公的突然过渡。你能谈谈为什么你认为现在进行这项研究很重要，你是如何进行的，以及你提出了哪些问题吗？是的，我想是在3月13日，当我们开始意识到很多人将要过渡到远程工作时，我联系了我在南佛罗里达大学的长期合作者塔米·艾伦，并说，我们是否应该尝试申请一笔快速资助来研究这个问题，因为这就像我们从未见过的自然实验，有这么多人转向远程工作。所以我们向国家科学基金会提交了一项研究建议。

5
00:02:47.000 --> 00:03:22.000
and I think we wrote it up within eight days and heard back seven days later that it was funded. So the whole process has been very rapid. And we set out really to explore best practices in remote work because there's been a good amount of research on it, but it's largely cross-sectional data comparing non-telecommuters to telecommuters. So what we wanted to dig into is within a sample of people who are all telecommuting, where you don't have some of these selection effects like managers picking people they want to telecommute.
我想我们在八天内就写好了申请，七天后就收到了获得资助的通知。所以整个过程非常迅速。我们真正着手探索的是远程工作的最佳实践，因为虽然已经有相当多的相关研究，但大多是横截面数据，比较的是非远程办公者和远程办公者。所以我们想深入研究的是，在一个全员远程办公的样本中，排除掉一些选择性效应，比如经理挑选他们想要远程办公的人。

6
00:03:22.000 --> 00:04:02.000
When everybody's in the kind of same situation, what are factors that vary day-to-day that might impact people's well-being as well as their productivity? So we just thought it was really just an unprecedented time to study these important issues and then be able to say we have some evidence-based recommendations moving forward to disseminate broadly. Do you have any results yet that you could talk about? So we just wrapped data collection up on Saturday. So we haven't analyzed anything formally. I did dig into the data a little bit just to look at some descriptive things. a few of the interesting things I saw where we asked people, and I should say the entire sample,
当每个人都处于同一种情况下，哪些因素会随时间变化并可能影响人们的幸福感和生产力？所以我们认为这是一个研究这些重要问题的绝佳时机，并能够提出一些基于证据的建议，以便广泛传播。你有什么可以分享的结果吗？我们刚刚在周六结束了数据收集。所以我们还没有进行任何正式的分析。我确实深入研究了一下数据，看了一些描述性的东西。我看到的一些有趣的事情是，我们询问了人们，我应该说整个样本，

7
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:46.000
they're all working fully remote, but we're not working remote very much at all, 10% or less before COVID. So we asked them, while COVID remains a public health threat, and what percent at the time would you want to stay working remote? And the vast majority said 100%. Wow. Yeah. I mean, I think it was 90% of the samples said 100%. That's pretty amazing. Yeah, that speaks to the fact that people are saying, you know, I don't want the risk. And even if this arrangement's not perfect, it's worth it to not, you know, expose potential exposure. We also asked once COVID is no longer as big of a threat, what percentage of the time would you like to work remote?
他们都在全职远程工作，但在新冠疫情之前，他们几乎没有远程工作过，比例在10%或以下。所以我们问他们，在新冠疫情仍然是公共卫生威胁的情况下，你希望有多少比例的时间保持远程工作？绝大多数人回答100%。哇。是的，我的意思是，我想样本中有90%的人回答了100%。这太令人惊讶了。是的，这说明人们在说，你知道，我不想冒风险。即使这种安排并不完美，为了不暴露在潜在的风险中也是值得的。我们还问，一旦新冠疫情不再是一个巨大的威胁，你希望有多少比例的时间远程工作？

